However, with this the tree looks awful:
![image](/uploads/2241624f447a784ecdd4b102de3e163f/image.png)

For every query attribute it add the type,flag and name.
Example:
```
Type: FIELD_TYPE_STRING (254)
Attribute Flags: 0x00
Attribute Name: string
```

And then it loops over the values and prints those:
```
Value (String): test test
```

The issues with this are:
1. There is no grouping, so it makes it difficult to see what type, flag and name belong together.
2. The Value is not added near the type/flag/name.
3. The decoding of date/time/datetime/timestamp is adding multiple items to the tree, which adds to the confusion. 

Example time value:
```
Length: 12
Flags: Positive (0)
Days: 0
Hour: 8
Minute: 23
Second: 24
Billionth of a second: 987654
```

@alagoutte I made this even more similar to prepared statement parameters. The result is that grouping, type names, etc now all work.

With the `mysql_queryattrs_all_types.pcapng.gz` file:

![image](/uploads/e84553b3cd6b7d0b279a6864dfa09779/image.png)

